---------------------------------- Building advanced directives --------------------------------------

- BUILD A FORM-LABEL DIRECTIVE USING HOST BINDING AND SIGNAL FORMS FIELD STATE.

- Instructions: Angular Prerequisite "Advanced components" chapter

- Bootstrap has a really good support for forms, and we started to use it. When we have a touched
    field in error, we add the is-invalid class to the input element to have a red border around it
    (🔥🔥done my [formField] directive). now we’d like to add a class text-danger on the label:

        <div class="mb-3">
            <label for="input-error-1" class="form-label 👉text-danger">Input with error</label>
            <input type="text" class="form-control is-invalid" id="input-error-1">
        </div>
    
    Of course, we only want to add this class if the field itself is in error and touched: sounds like
    the perfect job for a directive!

A DIRECTIVE TO HAVE A RED LABEL

1️⃣ We want to add a class on the label if the input next to it is invalid and touched. 🔥It’s easy
    to create a directive that will add the class depending on a condition if we give it the state
    of the field.

    🔥Let’s start by creating a directive called FormLabel that will add text-danger on the label
    if necessary:

        ng g d form-label



2️⃣ We want the directive to act on the label, so we could use a very broad selector like label.
    But that feels a bit too broad. So 🔥let’s choose a custom selector: label[prFormLabel]. That
    means we’ll have to add an attribute prFormLabel on the labels of our forms
    (add them in register.html and login.html).
    register.html:

        <form [formRoot]="userForm">
            <div class="mb-3">
                <label for="login" class="form-label" 👉[prFormLabel]="userForm.login">Login</label>
                // omitted
            </div>

            <div class="mb-3">
                <label for="password" class="form-label" 👉[prFormLabel]="userForm.password">Password</label>
               // omitted
            </div>

            <div class="mb-3">
                <label for="confirm" class="form-label" 👉[prFormLabel]="userForm.confirmPassword">Confirm password</label>
                // omitted
            </div>

            <div class="mb-3">
                <label for="birth-year" class="form-label" 👉[prFormLabel]="userForm.birthYear">Birth year</label>
               // omitted
            </div>
            // omitted
        </form>
    

    login.html:

        <form [formRoot]="credentials">
            <div class="mb-3">
                <label for="login" class="form-label" 👉[prFormLabel]="credentials.login">Login</label>
                // omitted
            </div>

            <div class="mb-3">
                <label for="password" class="form-label" 👉[prFormLabel]="credentials.password">Password</label>
                // omitted
            </div>
            // omitted
        </form>


    form-label.ts:

        @Directive({
            selector: 👉'label[prFormLabel]',
        })



3️⃣ The 🔥directive must have a single required input that receive the associated FieldTree<unknown>
    we want to watch. 🔥You can use an alias prFormLabel for that input, which will allow us to write
    in the template <label [prFormLabel]="userForm.login">. 🔥🔥Then use a host binding(see section 7
    of this chapter) that adds/removes the text-danger class depending on its touched status and
    validity.
    form-label.ts:

        @Directive({
            selector: 'label[prFormLabel]',
          👉host: {
                '[class.text-danger]': 'isInvalidAndTouched()'
            }
        })
        export class FormLabel {
          👉readonly fieldTree = input.required<FieldTree<unknown>>(👉{ alias: 'prFormLabel' });
          👉protected readonly isInvalidAndTouched = computed(() => {
                const fieldTree = this.fieldTree();
                const state = fieldTree();
                return state.touched() && state.invalid();
            })
        }



4️⃣ If you want it to be perfect, check that every label has the attribute 'prFormLabel' and every
    error message has the class 'invalid-feedback' in register.html and login.html.
    ✅sure we do for ef login.html:

        <form [formRoot]="credentials">
            <div class="mb-3">
                <label for="login" class="form-label" 👉[prFormLabel]="credentials.login">Login</label>
                <input id="login" class="form-control" [formField]="credentials.login" />
                <!-- error message here -->
                @let login = credentials.login();
                @if (login.touched() && !login.valid()) {
                    @if (login.getError('required')) {
                        <div 👉class="invalid-feedback">Login is required</div>
                    }
                }
            </div>

            <div class="mb-3">
                <label for="password" class="form-label" 👉[prFormLabel]="credentials.password">Password</label>
                <input id="password" type="password" class="form-control" [formField]="credentials.password" />
                <!-- error message here -->
                @let password = credentials.password();
                @if (password.touched() && !password.valid()) {
                    @if (password.getError('required')) {
                        <div 👉class="invalid-feedback">Password is required</div>
                    }
                }
            </div>
            // omitted
        </form>
    
    And we’re done!



